n do you think that people  do  hate being told what to do in ways like  that when they'll say but hang on a  minute we've got whole countries that  are  getting all their power from coal for  example what difference is it going to  make if i give up my burger  well nobody likes being told what to do  about anything but there's plenty of  opportunity in this as uh you know we're  talking about a  over a period of time a cultural change  and educational change a few decades ago  it used to be that for most people they  could probably become more healthy uh if  they eat more meat and dairy actually  that's not the case at the moment most  people would become  more healthy if they had less meat and  dairy  the taste and quality  of the  non-meat and dairy products that are on  the market at the moment you know are  going up and up and up so you know it's  just a question of changing habit a  little bit and i think many of the  underlying drivers that used to make  people used to give um status to meat  and dairy  are no longer relevant and actually a  lot of people if they understood more  about exactly how most of the meat and  dairy that we produce in the world  is produced actually would want less of  it so i think you know i think this  change is waiting to happen and i think  with the climate change committee's 35  target by 2035 i think they are  dramatically under-egging uh both the  opportunity and the need for deeper cuts  than that uh it doesn't have to be  instant okay and as we've just heard you  know it doesn't have to be a hundred  percent so there's nothing to be scared  about and most people find i think that  as they start to change their habits  their preferences change with them as  well and and  what do you think about this  conversation happening in in richer  western countries like our own i mean  it's a different matter for some poorer  countries isn't it to  start talking about veganism  yeah it's a matter of choice and i think  in lots of nations where the dominant  protein source is less easy to kind of  switch to a vegan alternative um i'm  thinking of places where there's a heavy  reliance on fish for instance i think  that would be a more challenging thing  and then there's also the kind of  climate justice angle where you you can  talk about okay in developed countries  we've got a lot of historical  responsibility for driving climate  change why should we then lump the  burden of lifestyle change onto  countries who have less historical  responsibility  but i think it's it's kind of a nuanced  argument i think as a  global society we need to make these  changes to  really accelerate the amount of climate  action that we are taking and absolutely  every single thing that we can throw at  the problem we should be and that  includes cutting fossil fuel use that  includes  switching to more plant-based diets and  that includes a whole suite of other  things that no doubt we will hear about  okay let's move on to our next topic  shall we because the uk isn't known for  its sunshine but the think tank ember  has argued that the uk could be  generating more solar power from this  renewable source and last year solar  power made up twice as much of the  netherlands electricity mix compared to  the uk despite having a similar number  of sunny days i probably not very many  so are we making the most of our  sunshine ella what do you think could we  be doing more as far as solar is  concerned do you think  absolutely  if you just have to look at the the  statistics and how much we're lagging  behind to make that judgment i would say  um i think the discussion around kind of  switching from  gas to renewables instead of taking coal  out of our electricity mix just  highlights that problem we need to make  a transition away from fossil fuels we  need to very very fast and it seems a  bit of a missed opportunity to not be  rolling out these kinds of proven  technologies that we have already at our  fingertips  but mike we are rolling out the proven  technology of wind power aren't we i  mean we  we  compared to our competitors or not our  competitors our neighbours i should say  in on the continent  we probably do much more as far as wind  power is concerned do we need to worry  about solar as well  well we need to press all the buttons as  hard as we can it's got to be a rapid  transition yes it's true we're doing a  bit better on wind compared to other  countries you know in relation to our  solar but it's also true to say we could  do a lot better with our solar so you  know if even if you just look at rooftop  solar it's such a shame when you see a  new house built and it's got a sunflow a  south facing roof and only half of that  roof is filled with solar panels what  was wrong with the other half you know  there's there are some simple things we  could do much better on solar it's also  true to say that for the uk actually the  balance of renewables between wind and  solar is going to be somewhat different  for than for other countries so if you  compare us to australia we have only one  200th of the sunlight per person  compared to australia but we've got  really good wins so there is a case for  a bit more wind compared to  compared to compared to solar relative  to some other countries but still we  could be pressing the button uh harder  on solar power and we absolutely should  despite the pressure on land that we've  got as a sort of you know quite densely  populated country with not brilliant  sunlight compared to some others we  could still do a lot more and how should  that happen is this about more subsidies  to encourage people to put solar panels  on their roof  i think yeah there should be some kind  of incentive to make it easy um i mean  i'm not a complete expert when it comes  to the policy and energy particularly  but it should be something that could be  accessible for for most people and i  think also for you know landlords to be  incentivized to put solar panels on  their roofs for councils to put it on  their social housing as well as relying  on homeowners to actually  see the benefits of themselves and put  them on their roofs i think it has to be  kind of a blanket across the whole  whole of society to incentivize the  rapid deployment of solar technologies  okay well we must leave it there dr ella  gilbert mike berners-lee always  fascinating to get your take on things  thanks very much indeed  so that's it from today but tomorrow  i'll be joined by fatima ibrahim from  the green new deal uk and co-founder of  global optimism tom rivet carnac but for  now thanks for watching i'll see you  next time  [Music]
 

